      "source": [
        "# Gaussian Integration by Parts & Stein's Lemma\n",
        "\n",
        "Recall that if $Z \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0,1)$, then for all functions $f\\in C^1(\\mathbb{R})$ such that $Z f(Z)$, $f(Z)$, and $f'(Z)$ are in $L^1$, we have:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\mathbb{E}[Z f(Z)] = \\mathbb{E}[f'(Z)].\n",
        "$$\n",
        "In fact, we do not need $f\\in C^1(\\mathbb{R})$  condition; it suffices that $f'$ exists almost everywhere (a.e.) and $f(t) = \\int_{-\\infty}^{t} f'(x) \\, dx$ for a.e. $t$ (which holds if and only if $f$ is absolutely continuous).\n",
        "\n",
        "The converse of this identity is also true and forms the foundation of Stein's method.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {#lem-stein-lemma}\n",
        "## Stein's Lemma\n",
        "Let $W$ be a random variable such that \n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\mathbb{E}[f'(W) - W f(W)] = 0,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "for all absolutely continuous functions $f$ with $\\sup |f'| < \\infty$. Then $W \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0,1)$.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Gaussian Tail Bounds\n",
        "\n",
        "Let $\\Phi(t) = F_Z(t) = \\mathbb{P}(Z \\le t)$ be the standard normal CDF.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {#lem-normal-tail-bound}\n",
        "## Normal Tail Bound\n",
        "For all $t > 0$, $\\Phi(-t) = 1 - \\Phi(t)$ satisfies:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\Phi(-t) = 1 - \\Phi(t) \\le \\min\\left\\{ \\frac{1}{2}, \\, \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi} \\, t} e^{-t^2/2}\\right\\}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        ":::{.proof}\n",
        "## Proof of @lem-normal-tail-bound\n",
        "Because $\\Phi(t) \\ge \\Phi(0) = 1/2$ for $t > 0$, we have $1 - \\Phi(t) \\le 1/2$. For $t > 0$, using the standard normal density $\\phi(x) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}} e^{-x^2/2}$:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "1 - \\Phi(t) = \\mathbb{P}(Z > t) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}} \\int_{t}^{\\infty} e^{-x^2/2} \\, dx\n",
        "$$\n",
        "For $x > t > 0$, we have $\\frac{x}{t} > 1$. Therefore:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\begin{align*}\n",
        "1 - \\Phi(t) &\\le \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi} \\, t} \\int_{t}^{\\infty} x e^{-x^2/2} \\, dx \\\\\n",
        "&= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi} \\, t} \\left[ -e^{-x^2/2} \\right]_{t}^{\\infty}\\\\\n",
        "& = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi} \\, t} e^{-t^2/2}.\n",
        "\\end{align*}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "Since both bounds hold simultaneously for all $t > 0$, their minimum is also a valid upper bound:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "1 - \\Phi(t) \\;\\le\\; \\min\\!\\left\\{ \\frac{1}{2},\\; \\frac{e^{-t^2/2}}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}\\,t} \\right\\}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "In the following, we use Python to visualize the normal tail bound. The plot shows the standard normal CDF $\\Phi(t)$, the tail probability $1 - \\Phi(t)$, and the upper bound $\\min\\{1/2, e^{-t^2/2}/(\\sqrt{2\\pi} t)\\}$ for $t > 0$."
      ],
